+## Canonical delivery protocol
+
+One request binds folder, sender actor epoch, receiver actor epoch, claim ID,
+attempt number, channel-binding digest, and one complete canonical operation.
+The operation dot must belong to the sender, so this version supports direct
+origin delivery rather than third-party gossip.
+
+One receipt binds the exact request digest, operation, claim, attempt, actors,
+channel, receiver evidence state, receiver generation, and receiver cutpoint.
+Inserted, pending, quarantined, and duplicate outcomes are evidence-terminal.
+`CapacityBlocked` is nonterminal and carries no fabricated retained evidence.
+Every receipt, including backpressure, must name a nonzero receiver generation.
+Unknown dispositions fail nonterminal and are rejected by validation.
+
+Frames contain exact magic/version/length/body plus a structural SHA-256 digest.
+Decode checks every field limit, consumes every byte, recomputes the digest, and
+requires canonical re-encoding equality. The structural digest detects malformed
+or changed bytes but is not authentication.
+
+Configuration validation computes worst-case request and receipt sizes. A wire
+policy cannot authorize an operation that its request frame can never carry, and
+it cannot permit receiver admission if no canonical receipt can fit. This avoids
+permanent claim/admission states caused by incoherent frame ceilings.
+
+## Delivery service and SQLite cutpoint
+
+`SyncReplicaDeliveryService` is the first non-test orchestration layer around the
+causal SQLite owner. It owns claim, request creation, receiver admission, receipt
+creation, and settlement, while a lower transport supplies authenticated peer
+and channel evidence.
+
+Receiver admission and receipt cutpoint are one authority decision.
+`accept_remote_with_cutpoint_or_throw()` captures admission, retained evidence
+state, generation, and cutpoint in the same SQLite write transaction. The service
+no longer admits in one transaction and snapshots in another.
+
+Wire-envelope limits are separate from mutable aggregate retention policy. A
+bounded operation can be decoded before the receiver classifies it as admitted,
+duplicate, capacity-blocked, or policy-invalid. Normal service operations also
+avoid redundant complete-owner snapshots that the first draft performed merely
+to recover limits.
+
+A sender process crash after transmission but before receipt application loses
+the in-memory expected request. The service does not reconstruct authority from
+receipt fields. Lease expiry creates a fresh claim and receiver idempotency
+returns an exact duplicate. A future durable response journal could reduce
+repeat work without weakening this rule.
+
+## TLS 1.3 boundary
+
+The OpenSSL adapter requires:
+
+- TLS 1.3 only;
+- peer verification enabled and successful;
+- client certificate authentication at the server;
+- exact ALPN `anonsync-replica-delivery-v1`;
+- a fresh, non-resumed session;
+- no early-data attempt;
+- exact SHA-256 pin of the peer certificate's DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo; and
+- an RFC 9266 `tls-exporter` binding derived with the standard label, empty
+  context, and 32-byte output.
+
+The caller supplies the actor epoch and pin from membership authority. The
+adapter does not infer folder membership from a generally trusted certificate.
+The current ALPN is private and unregistered; public interoperability is not
+claimed.
+
+Encrypted record I/O accepts only `SyncReplicaTlsAuthenticatedChannel`, a
+move-only capability constructed after all TLS and actor/SPKI checks. This
+prevents a caller from using raw `SSL*` record helpers while accidentally
+skipping the pin. Each record operation rechecks the live TLS security profile,
+peer SPKI, and exporter binding. Reusing the same `SSL` object for a fresh
+handshake therefore invalidates the old capability instead of silently binding
+old actor/channel authority to a new session. Record length is checked before
+allocation.
+
+## Owned clock and SQLite oracle
+
+Rev0875's truthful Linux clock capability model remains intact. Only an observed
+calling-thread time-namespace identity is bound. Missing or permission-hidden
+identity produces explicit synchronization `Unknown` evidence and sticky durable
+`synchronization-unknown` quarantine. Kernel generation is a diagnostic hint,
+not a capability proof.
